talk(568,'1982-10-04','A. A. Kirillov','P001045-P001047','Springer resolution and limiting orbital integrals',r'The entry occupies the middle and lower part of P001046 and the first lines of P001047. Kirillov reports Ginzburg\textquotesingle s work after explaining adjoint orbits and Springer resolution. The 1983 paper identifies the nilpotent-orbit limit formula precisely for complex semisimple algebras regarded as real. Gelfand then asks how a singular hyperbola integral recovers the value of a test function at the crossing.')
add(568,'Parameters of regular adjoint orbits','P001046','definition',r'\(\Omega_c\) is parametrized by \(\ell=\operatorname{rank}G\) invariants, illustrated by \(SL(2,\mathbb R)\) hyperboloids and traces of powers.',r'For a complex semisimple algebra, invariant polynomials parametrize the adjoint quotient, and regular semisimple fibers are single complex adjoint orbits. For real groups, invariant values alone can combine several real orbits. In \(\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb R)\), the negative and positive values of the invariant quadratic form give different hyperboloid types; the two sheets need not be one orbit.',r'The complex-as-real scope used by \citet[Sections~1--2]{ginzburg1983integrals} is narrower than the notebook\textquotesingle s initial arbitrary Lie group. The rank-one real qualification follows directly from the adjoint quadratic form.',('ginzburg1983integrals',),verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='The field, regular semisimple restriction and real-orbit distinction are supplied.')
add(568,'Springer incidence resolution','P001046','construction',r'For the unipotent variety \(N\), let \(\mathcal B\) be the Borel variety and \(\mathcal M=\{(n,B):n\in N,\ n\in B\}\); projection to \(N\) is called a desingularization.',r'For a connected complex semisimple algebraic group in characteristic zero, \(\widetilde N=G\times^B U_B\to N\) is proper and birational, with smooth source; its fiber over \(n\) is the Borel subgroups containing \(n\). In Lie-algebra form \(G\times^B\mathfrak n_B\simeq T^*(G/B)\) resolves the nilpotent cone. Properness and birationality, not just equal dimension and generic bijectivity, belong to the resolution statement.',r'This is the preexisting Springer framework cited by \citet{springer1976trigonometric} and used explicitly in \citet[Section~9]{ginzburg1983integrals}.',('springer1976trigonometric','ginzburg1983integrals'),verification='indirect',match='exact',reconstruction='Complex algebraic hypotheses, properness and the Lie-algebra/group distinction are explicit.')
add(568,'The real cylinder-to-cone illustration','P001046','construction',r'The drawing maps a cylinder to a double cone by \((x,y,z)\mapsto(xz,yz,z)\), collapsing its middle circle; it is identified with the \(SL(2,\mathbb R)\) example.',r'On \(x^2+y^2=1\), this map lands in \(X^2+Y^2=Z^2\), is one-to-one away from the cone vertex, and collapses exactly \(S^1\times\{0\}\). It models the topology of the real rank-one Springer map, whose exceptional fiber is \(\mathbb{RP}^1\simeq S^1\). The complex exceptional fiber is instead \(\mathbb{CP}^1\); the real drawing is not a description of all complex algebraic data.',r'The topology is checked directly from the displayed map and the flag fiber, within the Springer framework used by \citet[Sections~3,9]{ginzburg1983integrals}.',('ginzburg1983integrals',),literal='correct with missing hypotheses',verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='The source cylinder equation and the real versus complex interpretation are supplied.')
add(568,'Nilpotent orbital integrals from regular semisimple ones','P001046','theorem',r'\(\psi(c)=\int_{\Omega_c}f\), with coherently chosen measures; \(\int_{\Omega_0}f=(P\psi)(0)\), \(P\) a differential operator.',r'For a complex semisimple Lie algebra regarded as a real Lie algebra and a Schwartz test function \(f\), each nilpotent orbital integral is a constant multiple of a constant-coefficient differential operator applied at zero to the normalized Harish--Chandra orbital transform on a Cartan subalgebra. The operator polynomial is obtained from the corresponding Springer homology representation. Raw integrals in arbitrary quotient coordinates do not automatically satisfy the abbreviated identity.',r'\citet[Formula~(1), Sections~1--7]{ginzburg1983integrals} proves the Barbasch--Vogan conjectural formula in this scope. Presented 19 October 1982, received 23 November 1982 and published in 1983, it matches Kirillov\textquotesingle s report of another mathematician\textquotesingle s work; class B records that authorship.',('ginzburg1983integrals',),cl='B',verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='The group class, Schwartz decay, Cartan normalization, measure constants and orbit-dependent operator are restored.',status='the identified complex-group limit formula is established; no claim about all real reductive groups is inferred.')
add(568,'Recovering the crossing value from hyperbola integrals','P001047','question',r'Gelfand asks to recover \(f(0)\) from integrals over \(xy=c\), and refers to an expansion of \(\delta(xy-c)\) in the first volume of Generalized Functions.',r'For \(f\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^2)\), set \(I_f(c)=\int f(x,y)\delta(xy-c)\,dx\,dy\), \(c\ne0\). Then \(I_f(c)=-2f(0,0)\log|c|+O(1)\), so \(f(0,0)=\lim_{c\to0}I_f(c)/(-2\log|c|)\). This uses the delta/coarea measure, not ordinary arclength. Restricting to a single positive branch halves the leading coefficient.',r'The notes themselves identify the preexisting distribution-theoretic setting of \citet{gelfandshilov1964generalized}; only its bibliographic record was accessible. The stated leading term is verified here by writing \(I_f(c)=\int f(x,c/x)\,dx/|x|\), splitting near zero, and estimating the smooth remainder.',('gelfandshilov1964generalized',),verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',problem='GP-1982-04',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',reconstruction='Compact support, delta measure and the exact leading coefficient are supplied; this is an elementary reconstruction of the recorded question.')
add(568,'Why the singular limit contains logarithms','P001047','method',r'The text speaks of expanding \(\delta(xy-c)\) in powers of \(c\), then taking the hyperbola limit to obtain integrals over straight lines.',r'At the critical level \(c=0\), the pullback distribution cannot be handled by an ordinary Taylor expansion: \(d(xy)=0\) at the crossing. Asymptotics involve logarithmic terms and finite-part distributions supported on the axes. Even the constant term after subtracting \(-2f(0,0)\log|c|\) depends on the chosen regularization of the axis integrals.',r'The source allusion is to \citet{gelfandshilov1964generalized}; the leading logarithm above directly rules out an unqualified power-series or finite unrenormalized limit. The exact book passage was not located.',('gelfandshilov1964generalized',),literal='false as written',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The need for logarithmic/finite-part terms is an explicit mathematical correction, without claiming an unread full asymptotic formula.')
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1982-04',title='Recover a crossing value from hyperbola orbital integrals',statement=r'For compactly supported smooth \(f\) on \(\mathbb R^2\), recover \(f(0,0)\) from the family \(\int f(x,y)\delta(xy-c)\,dx\,dy\), \(c\ne0\).',occurrences=['1982-568.5'],status=r'As of November 2026, an elementary solved singular-asymptotic problem: the leading coefficient is \(-2f(0,0)\log|c|\). The notebook already refers to the older generalized-functions treatment.',status_date='2026-11',refs=['gelfandshilov1964generalized']))
corr('P001046','Resolution definition and real illustration',r'Generic bijectivity and equal dimension alone define desingularization; the cylinder picture is said to be exactly the Springer example.',r'An algebraic resolution is proper birational with smooth source; retain the cylinder as a real topological rank-one model and distinguish complex exceptional fibers.','Scans1046--47 inspected. Incidence projection is proper because the Borel variety is projective. The real exceptional fiber is RP1, the complex fiber CP1.',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001047','Power expansion and limit of hyperbola integrals',r'Expansion of \(\delta(xy-c)\) in powers of c; direct limiting axis integral.',r'Include logarithmic terms and finite-part axis distributions; the leading full-plane term is \(-2\log|c|\,\delta_{(0,0)}\).','Scan1047 checked. Direct integration f(x,c/x)/|x| with f constant near0 gives the logarithmic divergence, so a plain Taylor series is impossible.')
audit.append('## Record 568\nRussian1046--first1047 read; scans1046 and1047 fully inspected (cylinder/cone, hyperbola branches and orbital formula). Exact primary identification: Ginzburg1983 dan10001 full4-page Russian PDF opened successfully with option_lang=rus, sections1--7 and9 read; formula1 is for complex semisimple algebra viewed as real and normalized Harish-Chandra transform, not arbitrary real Lie groups. Presented19Oct1982, received23Nov1982, published1983; authorship is Ginzburg, so Kirillov report classB. Springer1976 publisher metadata opened, original full text not read. Gelfand--Shilov1964 English catalogue at CiNii opened; exact book page unavailable. Primary/institutional bibliographic lookup distinguished1958 Russian from1964 English. Direct derivation of -2f(0)log|c| and coefficient retrieval. Earlier index search found related Springer geometry but no exact hyperbola problem identity. No open problem manufactured from a known singular limit.')
